
# Cisco Static Routing, Administrative Distance (AD) & Network Redundancy Lab

## Project Overview

This project demonstrates the implementation of **Static Routing, Administrative Distance (AD), Floating Static Routes, Default Routing, and Network Redundancy** using Cisco Packet Tracer.

The purpose of this lab is to understand how routers communicate across multiple networks, how routing tables work, how Cisco routers select the best route, and how backup routes can maintain network connectivity when a primary connection fails.

The network consists of a central router, two branch routers (BR1 and BR2), an ISP router, switches, PCs, a laptop, and a server.

This project focuses on practical network configuration, routing decisions, connectivity verification, and troubleshooting using the Cisco IOS Command Line Interface (CLI).

---

## Network Topology

![Network Topology](topology.png)

The topology includes:

- **Core Router:** The central router connecting local networks, branch routers, and the ISP.
- **BR1:** Branch router connecting the 10.10.30.0/24 network to the core network.
- **BR2:** Branch router connecting the 10.10.40.0/24 network to the core network.
- **ISP Router:** Provides an upstream connection.
- **Switches:** Provide Layer 2 communication within individual LANs.
- **Laptop0:** A client connected to the 10.10.10.0/24 network.
- **Server0:** A server connected to the 10.10.20.0/24 network.
- **PC2:** A client connected to the BR1 LAN.
- **PC1:** A client connected to the BR2 LAN.

BR1 and BR2 are directly connected, providing an alternative path between the branches and the central network.

This connection makes it possible to demonstrate network redundancy using floating static routes.

### IP Addressing Table

| Network | Subnet | Description |
|---|---|---|
| Laptop LAN | 10.10.10.0/24 | Laptop network |
| Server LAN | 10.10.20.0/24 | Server network |
| BR1 LAN | 10.10.30.0/24 | Branch 1 network |
| BR2 LAN | 10.10.40.0/24 | Branch 2 network |
| Core–BR1 | 150.150.150.0/24 | Primary connection to BR1 |
