## 8. Network Redundancy

Network redundancy means providing multiple possible communication paths to improve availability.

In this topology, BR1 and BR2 are directly connected.

Both branch routers also have their own connections to the Core router.

This creates alternative paths for reaching remote networks.

### Primary Path

```text
BR1 → Core Router → Server LAN
```

### Backup Path

```text
BR1 → BR2 → Core Router → Server LAN
```

If the direct BR1-to-Core connection fails, traffic may be redirected through BR2 using a correctly configured floating static route.

### Benefits of Redundancy

- Improved network reliability.
- Increased availability.
- Reduced downtime during supported failure scenarios.
- Alternative forwarding paths.
- Better fault tolerance.

A complete redundant network requires both forward and return routes to be configured correctly.

