## 10. Understanding the Routing Table

The routing table stores information that routers use to forward packets.

To display the IPv4 routing table:

```cisco
show ip route
```

### Common Routing Table Codes

| Code | Meaning |
|---|---|
| C | Connected |
| L | Local |
| S | Static |
| S* | Candidate Default Static Route |
| R | RIP |
| O | OSPF |
| D | EIGRP |
| B | BGP |

Example:

```text
C 10.10.30.0/24 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/0
L 10.10.30.1/32 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/0
S 10.10.20.0/24 [1/0] via 150.150.150.1
S 10.10.10.0/24 [1/0] via 150.150.150.1
```

### Understanding [1/0]

```text
[Administrative Distance / Metric]
```

In this example:

- Administrative Distance: 1
- Metric: 0

The route is a static route using the default AD value.

